  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough next Sunday...\n\nShortwave which evolved out of remnants of Hurricane Rachel will\nlift to the north early this morning, and precipitation will taper\noff accordingly from SW to NE. At the same time, as surface trof \nshifts east, a period of breezy NW winds will surge across the \neast half of the cwa, with highest gusts (30-40 mph) expected at \nSheridan along the favored eastern slopes of the Bighorns. Winds \nwill decrease thru the afternoon. A few showers may eke into the \nfar south this afternoon per some instability and backing mid \nlevel winds as the cut off low remains over the great basin. \nTonight will be chilly with a good chance of a freeze and possible\nfog over much of the area, though these items will depend on at \nleast partial clearing across lower elevations.\n\nUpper low will move slowly eastward through the central Rockies \nover the next two days, eventually reaching the central plains \nThursday. Proximity to the low results in a chance (20-40%) of \nrain/snow showers over the southern mountains/foothills and \nperhaps into far southeast MT Tuesday & Wednesday. Models have \ntrended tracking the low a bit further north so confidence for \nthis has increased some. Expect snow levels to waver between \n6-9kft during this time, so lower elevations should see just rain \nshowers while the mountains see snow/rain showers. Most of the cwa\nwill remain dry Tuesday to Thursday, with temps climbing to the \n50s to near 60F.\n\nCanadian clipper drops into the northern plains Thursday, but it \nis looking more likely to be a dry feature, with impacts being \nsome cooling and breezy NW winds over the east. That said, \nensemble means show a 1035+mb surface high settling over eastern \nMT and the western Dakotas Thursday night. Eastern parts may see \nthe coldest night of the week Thursday night with a hard freeze \npossible.\n\nHigh confidence exists for a building western CONUS ridge and\nwarmer/drier weather (with breezy downslope winds) Friday through\nSunday. Look for highs back into the 70s by the weekend. JKL",
